# <Product Name> - HOOPS AI Embedded Redistributable Package (Sample README Template)

This file is a sample README to be bundled into the ZIP produced by the
"Creating a Redistributable Package" procedure below. In an actual
distribution, replace `<Product Name>` with your real product name and add
your own support contact information.

This package bundles the binaries, the full Python runtime, and the trained
model(s) required to run the native application (`<Product Name>`) that
embeds HOOPS AI. The license key is embedded into the binary at build time,
so no license key configuration is required on the end-user side.

## Contents

- `bin/` — Built native binaries (executable / shared library)
- `.venv/` — The minimal set of Python packages required to run HOOPS AI,
  laid out using the same convention as a normal installation
  (Linux: `.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/`, Windows: `.venv\Lib\site-packages\`)
  (training/development-only packages are not included)
- `models/` — Trained model(s) (.ckpt)
- `samples/` — Sample data for verification (if needed)

`test_client` auto-detects the bundled `.venv` relative to its own location,
so it is run directly (`bin/test_client` on Linux, `bin\test_client.exe` on
Windows) without any launcher wrapper or `HOOPS_AI_HOME` setting.

## End-user environment prerequisites (not included in this package)

- **Python 3.12 runtime** (same minor version used at build time, providing
  `libpython3.12.so.1.0` (Linux) / `python312.dll` (Windows). The `-dev`
  package or development headers for Python itself are not required —
  only the runtime is needed. On Windows, install it with the python.org
  installer so it is registered in the PEP 514 registry; the bridge then
  auto-detects it and no `HOOPS_AI_PYTHON_HOME` needs to be set)
- The OS's standard C/C++ runtime (on Linux, `libstdc++`/`libgcc`, usually
  preinstalled)
- **For headless (no display) Linux servers**: Xvfb and OpenGL-related
  libraries (`libglu1-mesa` `libgl1` `libosmesa6` `xvfb`). See the main
  README for details.
- The same CPU architecture (e.g., x86_64), OS, and Python build (ABI) as
  this package

## Usage (verification commands for the packaging engineer)

> **This section shows example commands for the packaging engineer to
> verify that the distributed package works, not commands for the
> end user to run directly. In an actual product, do not hand
> `test_client` to the end user — replace it with `<Product Name>`'s own
> UI (menu operations, etc.).**

**Windows** (cmd.exe or PowerShell):
```
cd redist_package
rem HOOPS_AI_PYTHON_HOME is optional: if unset, the Python 3.12 install is
rem auto-detected from the PEP 514 registry. Set it only for unusual setups
rem where auto-detection fails:
rem set HOOPS_AI_PYTHON_HOME=<path to your Python 3.12 installation>
bin\test_client.exe mfr          samples\sample_a.step models\<mfr_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin\test_client.exe embed        samples\sample_a.step models\<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin\test_client.exe compare      samples\sample_a.step samples\sample_b.step models\<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin\test_client.exe index-add    samples\sample_a.step models\<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin\test_client.exe index-search samples\sample_a.step 5 models\<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin\test_client.exe index-info
```
> In PowerShell, set environment variables with `$env:VAR = "value"` instead
> of `set VAR=value`.

**Linux**:
```bash
cd redist_package
# On a headless (no display) server, start Xvfb and export DISPLAY first
# (see the main README); e.g.:
#   Xvfb :99 -screen 0 1280x960x24 & export DISPLAY=:99
bin/test_client mfr          samples/sample_a.step models/<mfr_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin/test_client embed        samples/sample_a.step models/<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin/test_client compare      samples/sample_a.step samples/sample_b.step models/<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin/test_client index-add    samples/sample_a.step models/<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin/test_client index-search samples/sample_a.step 5 models/<embeddings_checkpoint>.ckpt
bin/test_client index-info
```

## Notes

- This package only runs on an environment whose CPU architecture, OS, and
  Python ABI version match the environment it was built on.
- The contents of `.venv/` were created based on a measured list of modules
  obtained by actually running the features this product uses (in this
  case, MFR inference and shape embedding computation). If additional
  features are used in the future, you must re-run the module trace after
  actually exercising the newly added feature.
- For third-party libraries (including HOOPS AI/HOOPS Exchange), only use
  redistribution/licensing terms that have been separately confirmed under
  the specific conditions of your TPA (partner agreement) with Tech Soft 3D.
  Also refer to the official Acknowledgments page for the list of
  dependency licenses:
  https://docs.techsoft3d.com/hoops/ai/resources/Acknowledgments.html
